<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:media="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Media;assembly=PresentationCore">

    <!--
        THE LIST OF WHAT CAN BE WRITTEN UNDER THE SEARCH BOX, 2026-09-15. A sixteenth theme file
        rather than more lines in Controls.xaml, which stood at 328 against a ceiling of 376 with
        three templates and their reasons to add. The seam is the one Menus.xaml took: Controls.xaml
        calls itself "the field somebody types into, the box somebody ticks, the chip", and a
        floating list is none of those. Not one value of its own - ADR-23, the same rule as every
        other file here.

        OWN TEMPLATES RATHER THAN BasedOn THEIRS, measured: WPF UI registers implicit styles for
        ListBox AND ListBoxItem, and their item template answers hover and selection in their
        brushes through their triggers - the same shape as trap 6 in `docs/10`. Every family this
        product invents owns its template since 2026-09-01, and this is one.

        ORDER IS LOAD BEARING: the row's styles are BasedOn FixedText and BodySubduedText from
        Text.xaml through StaticResource, so this file merges after that one. It merges after Menus.xaml in App.xaml,
        beside the other floating surface, and nothing below it names anything declared here.
    -->

    <!--
        THE SURFACE THE LIST STANDS ON: the panel colour with the panel edge on all four sides,
        which is what the details panel and the plan sheet already are. A Border of its own rather
        than the ListBox's, so the sentence about the keys under the list stands on the same
        surface without a seam through it.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="SuggestionPanel" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfacePanel}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource SurfacePanelEdge}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource BorderAllSides}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        WHAT LEADS AND WHAT FOLLOWS, 2026-09-16. A completion leads with the word, because the
        person is looking for the thing they started typing. A question - one of the examples on an
        empty box - leads with the sentence, because the person has not typed anything and does not
        know the language yet: the sentence is what they can read, and the syntax follows it and
        teaches itself. The row model says which is which (Lead and Trail) and these two styles
        swap the face to match, so syntax is always in the fixed face and a sentence always in the
        reading face, whichever column it stands in. ONE SIZE FOR BOTH: the owner's photograph had
        a white 13 word with a grey 12 sentence glued to it, and the two sizes in one line were
        half of "looks terrible". The other half was the glue - MarginSuggestionTrail.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="SuggestionLeadText" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource FixedText}">
        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding IsQuestion}" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="SuggestionTrailText" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource BodySubduedText}">
        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding IsQuestion}" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFaceFixed}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        ONE ROW: what leads and what follows it - the two styles above say which is in which face.
        A DataTemplate rather than the row drawn
        inside the item's ControlTemplate, and `docs/10` trap 26 is the reason: a TextBlock in a
        DataTemplate keeps its node in the automation tree, a TextBlock in a ControlTemplate does
        not - so a reader from outside gets the meaning as well as the word.

        The lead column is shared across rows so the trails line up down the list - the trail is
        trimmed rather than wrapped because a row that grows taller moves the rows under the
        pointer.
    -->
    <DataTemplate x:Key="SuggestionRow">
        <Grid>
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="SuggestionLead" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding Lead}" Style="{StaticResource SuggestionLeadText}" />
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                       Text="{Binding Trail}"
                       Style="{StaticResource SuggestionTrailText}"
                       Margin="{StaticResource MarginSuggestionTrail}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
        </Grid>
    </DataTemplate>

    <!--
        THE KEYS UNDER THE LIST STAND IN A FOOT OF THEIR OWN, 2026-09-16: a rule above them, the
        same one the plan sheet draws between its body and its footer, so the sentence about the
        keys reads as furniture and not as one more row.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="SuggestionFoot" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource SurfacePanelEdge}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource BorderFooterRule}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadCell}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE LIST ITSELF, capped at the same height as the column picker's menu - one decision, "a
        floating list may not be taller than this", written once in HeightMenu rather than twice.
        Not focusable and not a tab stop, and the style says so rather than relying on the popup: a
        ListBox is both by default. No surface of its own - the Border around it carries that.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="SuggestionList" TargetType="ListBox">
        <Setter Property="Focusable" Value="False" />
        <Setter Property="IsTabStop" Value="False" />
        <Setter Property="MaxHeight" Value="{StaticResource HeightMenu}" />
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="ListBox">
                    <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                                  HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                                  Focusable="False">
                        <ItemsPresenter />
                    </ScrollViewer>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        ONE ITEM: hover in the hover surface FOR THE PANEL, the chosen one in the selected surface.
        Hover was the window's brush until 2026-09-16, and on this surface that brush reads 1.30 -
        under the floor of 1.5, and exactly the value the floor was chosen to refuse - so the rows
        did not answer the pointer and did not look like anything one could click. The argument is
        beside SurfaceHoverOnPanel. The selected brush is the one the list's row wears, so a chosen
        thing has one colour in the window: it reads 2.36 here against the 3.0 a state answers to on
        the window, and a fill alone cannot do better on this surface - a fill 3.0 from #343434 is
        too light for white text at 4.5. Measured, written down, and a decision rather than a fix.
        Not focusable, or the first click
        in the popup would take the keyboard out of the box and close the popup under the pointer.
        The name for automation is the WORD, so a probe reads what a person would type.

        Brushes.Transparent on the face is deliberate and is `docs/10` trap 11: a transparent brush
        takes the pointer and no brush does not, so without it hover would work over the letters
        only. Selected is declared AFTER hover so that it wins on the one row that is both.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="SuggestionItem" TargetType="ListBoxItem">
        <Setter Property="Focusable" Value="False" />
        <Setter Property="IsTabStop" Value="False" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadCell}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
        <Setter Property="AutomationProperties.Name" Value="{Binding Word}" />
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="ListBoxItem">
                    <Border x:Name="Face"
                            Background="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}"
                            Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
                        <ContentPresenter />
                    </Border>
                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceHoverOnPanel}" />
                        </Trigger>
                        <Trigger Property="IsSelected" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceSelected}" />
                        </Trigger>
                        <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>
</ResourceDictionary>
